### What steps can reproduce the bug?

When throwing a `HTTPException` from a `catch` block, changes made in the `finally` block aren't applied to the response.

For example, deleting cookies in a `finally` block

```ts
import { deleteCookie } from 'hono/cookie'
import { HTTPException } from 'hono/http-exception'

app.use(async (c) => {
  try {
    await maybeError()
  } catch (cause) {
    throw new HTTPException(302, {
      cause,
      res: c.redirect('/?error=invalid_request', 302),
    })
  } finally {
    deleteCookie(c, 'foo')
  }
})
```

### What is the expected behavior?

`try...catch...finally` is a handy way to clean up after an exception, eg;

```ts
try {
  // perform
} catch {
  // handle error
} finally {
  // cleanup
}
```

So I'd expect to see a `set-cookie` header included in the redirect response.

### What do you see instead?

The redirect response does not include a `set-cookie` header

### Additional information

From what I understand, this happens because `c.redirect()` has already created a response using headers that were set before `deleteCookie()` was called. A simpler example of the issue is..

```ts
const response = c.redirect()
deleteCookie(c, 'foo')
response.headers.get('set-cookie') // null
```

I'm sure there are other ways of producing the same result, I initially found this a little surprising, so I had to do some digging to understand why it was happening.

I can work around this by either doing the cleanup twice..

```ts
try {
  await maybeError()
  cleanup()
} catch {
  cleanup()
  throw new HTTPException()
}
```
..or rearranging things so I do the cleanup first..
```ts
try {
  cleanup()
  await maybeError()
} catch {
  throw new HTTPException()
}
```

It's just unfortunate the this part of Hono doesn't work will with regular javascript syntax like the `try...catch...finally` statement

